  "readings": [
    "The float lead is constant in the denominator for every seed except 13 (0.9602557582657572 at d4-d32, 0.9634775563081881 at d64): the optimizer stack is seeded identically and the lambda grid does not change the float search, only which rationals the lead can be snapped onto.",
    "Seed 5 is the ONLY seed whose float lead exceeds 1 (1.001451519478135), and it did so already at denominator 4. Denominators 8, 16, 32 and 64 did not find new geometry; they raised the resolution of the exact certificate until the lead could be certified. The kill was therefore visible in the first seed-5 run and took five denominators to become admissible evidence.",
    "Convergence of the exact ratio to the float lead is NOT monotone in general: seed 1 falls from 0.8928950 (d32) to 0.8920101 (d64), and seed 13 falls from 0.9561767 (d16) to 0.9527880 (d32) before returning to 0.9561767 (d64). Each denominator is an independent snapping attempt, not a refining limit. Seed 5 happens to be monotone.",
    "Nine of the ten seeds saturate strictly below 1 at their own ceiling; the ceilings are per-cell and range from 0.744 (seed 21) to 0.956 (seed 13). There is no universal raw ceiling near 0.89, which is what the seed 1-2 pair suggested in addendum B Section 95.",
    "Addendum B Section 98.4 is settled: clean seed-13 runs with --snap coefficients reach 0.9561767406098319 at d16 and d64 and 0.9527879513477887 at d32, all below the 0.9585 obtained with --snap both. The projection branch of the snapper did add roughly 0.0023 over every clean seed-13 value, so the original demotion reason was correct in direction; the magnitude first attributed to it (the gap to 0.8903) was not, because that gap is seed variation."
